DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssiiggttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() system call is equivalent to ssiiggwwaaiittiinnffoo() except that
     if none of the signals specified by _s_e_t are pending, ssiiggttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() waits
     for the time interval specified in the _t_i_m_e_s_p_e_c structure referenced by
     _t_i_m_e_o_u_t.  If the _t_i_m_e_s_p_e_c structure pointed to by _t_i_m_e_o_u_t is zero-valued
     and if none of the signals specified by _s_e_t are pending, then
     ssiiggttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() returns immediately with an error.  If _t_i_m_e_o_u_t is the NULL
     pointer, ssiiggttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() blocks indefinitely.  CLOCK_MONOTONIC clock is
     used to measure the time interval specified by the _t_i_m_e_o_u_t argument.

     The ssiiggwwaaiittiinnffoo() system call selects the pending signal from the set
     specified by _s_e_t.  Should any of multiple pending signals in the range
     SIGRTMIN to SIGRTMAX be selected, it shall be the lowest numbered one.
     The selection order between realtime and non-realtime signals, or between
     multiple pending non-realtime signals, is unspecified.  If no signal in
     _s_e_t is pending at the time of the call, the calling thread is suspended
     until one or more signals in _s_e_t become pending or until it is inter‐
     rupted by an unblocked, caught signal.

     The ssiiggwwaaiittiinnffoo() system call is equivalent to the ssiiggwwaaiitt() system call
     if the _i_n_f_o argument is NULL.  If the _i_n_f_o argument is non-NULL, the
     ssiiggwwaaiittiinnffoo() function is equivalent to ssiiggwwaaiitt(), except that the se‐
     lected signal number shall be stored in the _s_i___s_i_g_n_o member, and the
     cause of the signal shall be stored in the _s_i___c_o_d_e member.  Besides this,
     the ssiiggwwaaiittiinnffoo() and ssiiggttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() system calls may return EINTR if in‐
     terrupted by signal, which is not allowed for the ssiiggwwaaiitt() function.

     If any value is queued to the selected signal, the first such queued
     value is dequeued and, if the info argument is non-NULL, the value is
     stored in the _s_i___v_a_l_u_e member of _i_n_f_o.  The system resource used to queue
     the signal is released and returned to the system for other use.  If no
     value is queued, the content of the _s_i___v_a_l_u_e member is zero-valued.  If
     no further signals are queued for the selected signal, the pending indi‐
     cation for that signal is reset.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     Upon successful completion (that is, one of the signals specified by _s_e_t
     is pending or is generated) ssiiggwwaaiittiinnffoo() and ssiiggttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() return the
     selected signal number.  Otherwise, the functions return a value of -1
     and set the global variable _e_r_r_n_o to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
     The ssiiggttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() system call will fail if:

     [EAGAIN]           No signal specified by set was generated within the
                        specified timeout period.

     The ssiiggttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() and ssiiggwwaaiittiinnffoo() system calls fail if:

     [EINTR]            The wait was interrupted by an unblocked, caught sig‐
                        nal.

     The ssiiggttiimmeeddwwaaiitt() system call may also fail if:

     [EINVAL]           The _t_i_m_e_o_u_t argument specified a _t_v___n_s_e_c value less
                        than zero or greater than or equal to 1000 million.
                        Kernel only checks for this error if no signal is
                        pending in set and it is necessary to wait.

